WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.128
Review a TypeScript application's
credential design as a lifecycle.

2
00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:08.043
Begin with the capabilities it needs, the
identity that should

3
00:00:08.043 --> 00:00:11.767
exercise them, and the resources that
identity may reach.

4
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.058
Follow each credential from provisioning
through

5
00:00:14.058 --> 00:00:16.827
delivery, runtime use, renewal, and
revocation.

6
00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:19.119
This reveals unnecessary copies and
authority

7
00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.601
that a storage-only review can miss.

8
00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:24.084
Treat reusable secrets as portable
authority.

9
00:00:24.084 --> 00:00:26.757
Eliminate permanent application keys where
workload identity

10
00:00:26.757 --> 00:00:29.526
is supported, and let platform credential
providers

11
00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:32.486
obtain and refresh narrowly scoped,
short-lived credentials.

12
00:00:32.486 --> 00:00:34.873
Identity establishes the requesting
workload; authorization

13
00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:36.973
remains a separate least-privilege
decision.

14
00:00:36.973 --> 00:00:40.411
A service identity is not a reason to
grant

15
00:00:40.411 --> 00:00:44.134
administrative access, and temporary
credentials remain sensitive while valid.

16
00:00:44.134 --> 00:00:46.521
For credentials that remain necessary,
managed

17
00:00:46.521 --> 00:00:49.386
storage controls custody without
eliminating the secret.

18
00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:52.441
Prefer workload identity to solve the
store's authentication

19
00:00:52.441 --> 00:00:55.401
bootstrap, then restrict access to
specific secrets.

20
00:00:55.401 --> 00:00:57.788
Environment variables deliver
configuration; they do

21
00:00:57.788 --> 00:01:00.270
not supply secret management by
themselves.

22
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.039
Keep confidential values out of source
control,

23
00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:06.190
logs, images, build artifacts, and browser
bundles.

24
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.577
Secret scanning detects some mistakes,
while

25
00:01:08.577 --> 00:01:11.441
prevention reduces the opportunities to
make them.

26
00:01:11.441 --> 00:01:13.924
Design rotation together with consumer
refresh.

27
00:01:13.924 --> 00:01:16.597
Replacing a stored value and making a

28
00:01:16.597 --> 00:01:19.462
running process use it are distinct
operations.

29
00:01:19.462 --> 00:01:21.944
Bound caches, renew expiring credentials,
and

30
00:01:21.944 --> 00:01:24.809
account for connection pools and
revocation behavior.

31
00:01:24.809 --> 00:01:27.959
Use bounded failure handling when
dependencies are unavailable,

32
00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:31.588
without falling back to permanent or more
privileged credentials.

33
00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:34.739
Keep developer identity distinct from
workload identity, and

34
00:01:34.739 --> 00:01:38.367
separate development, test, staging,
production, and deployment authority.

35
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:41.518
Verify provider precedence and the
effective principal, then

36
00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:44.764
test denied access as carefully as
successful requests.

37
00:01:44.764 --> 00:01:47.819
Assign ownership for renewal and incident
response so

38
00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:50.684
that the design remains operable after
release.

39
00:01:50.684 --> 00:01:54.121
The strongest secret is often the one the
application

40
00:01:54.121 --> 00:01:57.176
no longer needs because the runtime can
prove

41
00:01:57.176 --> 00:02:00.900
its identity and obtain narrowly scoped,
short-lived authority instead.

